  <h2>Side-by-Side Calculation Example: $400,000 Cleveland Rental</h2>
  <p>Let's calculate cap rate and cash-on-cash return on the same property to show exactly how they differ. Property: 3-bed/2-bath Cleveland duplex, purchase price $400,000, market rent $2,800/month per unit ($5,600 total).</p>
  <div class="table-responsive">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Gross Annual Rent</td><td>$67,200</td><td>$5,600 × 12</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Vacancy (7%)</td><td>– $4,704</td><td>Cleveland market avg</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Property Management (9%)</td><td>– $5,623</td><td>Industry standard for duplex</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Insurance</td><td>– $2,400</td><td>$200/month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Property Tax</td><td>– $5,600</td><td>1.4% effective rate in Cuyahoga Co.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Repairs &amp; Maintenance</td><td>– $4,000</td><td>1% of value rule-of-thumb</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Net Operating Income (NOI)</strong></td><td><strong>$44,873</strong></td><td>Gross rent minus all operating expenses</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Purchase Price</td><td>$400,000</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cap Rate</strong></td><td><strong>11.2%</strong></td><td>$44,873 ÷ $400,000</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Now add financing to get Cash-on-Cash Return. Assuming 25% down ($100,000) and 7.25% mortgage on $300,000 over 30 years:</p>
  <div class="table-responsive">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>NOI</td><td>$44,873</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Annual Mortgage Payment (P+I)</td><td>– $24,553</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Annual Pre-Tax Cash Flow</strong></td><td><strong>$20,320</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cash Invested (down + closing costs)</td><td>$112,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cash-on-Cash Return</strong></td><td><strong>18.1%</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Notice how CoC (18.1%) is significantly <em>higher</em> than cap rate (11.2%) in this case. That's <strong>positive leverage</strong> — your mortgage rate (7.25%) is lower than your cap rate (11.2%), so every dollar you borrow amplifies your return on invested equity.</p>

